### PART VII — ADVANCED VULNERABILITY RESEARCH

### Module 27: Binary vulnerability classes

This is where you begin connecting binary knowledge to actual security defects.

| Vulnerability        | What you must understand                            |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Buffer overflow      | Data exceeds allocated buffer                       |
| Integer overflow     | Arithmetic exceeds a fixed-width range              |
| Integer underflow    | Arithmetic goes below an allowed range              |
| Integer truncation   | Value loses information when converted              |
| Signedness bug       | Signed and unsigned values are handled incorrectly  |
| Out-of-bounds read   | Program reads outside valid memory                  |
| Out-of-bounds write  | Program writes outside valid memory                 |
| Use-after-free       | Freed memory is accessed again                      |
| Double free          | Memory is released more than once                   |
| Heap corruption      | Heap metadata or memory is damaged                  |
| Stack corruption     | Stack data is overwritten                           |
| Format string bug    | Unsafe format-string handling                       |
| Type confusion       | Incompatible data types are interpreted incorrectly |
| Uninitialized memory | Uninitialized data is used                          |
| Race condition       | Unexpected timing affects program behaviour         |
| Deserialization flaw | Encoded data is reconstructed unsafely              |
| Parser differential  | Two components interpret the same data differently  |

For each vulnerability, study:

* Root cause.

* Binary representation involved.

* Conditions required.

* Program behaviour.

* Reproduction in a local lab.

* Impact.

* Detection.

* Mitigation.

* Responsible disclosure.

